      "role": "user",
      "content": "i tried the blank workbook like u said and it actually worked fine there. so the issue is definitely something hidden in the original file. i've checked the add-ins and there's nothing unusual. i feel like i'm chasing ghosts here lol. why would a specific file behave so badly out of nowhere?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a specific file starts acting up after working fine, it's often due to accumulated data or hidden objects that are being triggered by the routine. Think of it like a pile of old, sticky bits in the corner of the workshop that only cause trouble when a certain heavy tool—your macro—is used. Sometimes, a single, stray comment or a hidden formatting object in one of the cells within that `A1:Z100` range can cause the object model to choke when `.ClearContents` tries to sweep everything away.\n\nSince you've isolated it to the original file, try this: before running `ws.Range(\"A1:Z100\").ClearContents`, add a line to specifically check for and delete any shapes or charts within that range. A rogue, invisible chart object can sometimes throw this exact error when the code tries to clear the contents. You could add something like `ws.Range(\"A1:Z100\").SpecialCells(xlCellTypeShapes).Delete` before your clear command.",
      "source": "target",
